course: everyday
lesson: 320
title: Interrupting, boasting, your own story, losing a pet or a person, bullying, saying no, comparing yourself online, pocket money and curiosity
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good friends let others finish speaking and share the fun instead of boasting. When you are excited, wait for a pause; when you have more toys, invite others to play.
explain: Some things are yours to decide: if you are adopted or in foster care, you share only as much of your story as you want.
explain: Losing a pet or a person hurts. Death means the body has stopped working forever, so the one who died cannot come back; it is not like sleeping. Grief takes time, and a new pet does not replace the old one.
explain: Some rules keep you safe even under pressure: tell a trusted adult about bullying even if the bully says not to, and say «No thanks» to cigarettes, vapes and alcohol.
explain: Online pictures show only people's best moments. Real life has ups and downs for everyone, so comparing yourself with them is unfair to you.
fact: When you are excited and want to speak, wait for a pause in the talk, because people feel respected when they can finish.
fact: Boasting about having more than others makes them feel small and pushes friends away; sharing the fun brings friends closer.
fact: If someone asks about your adoption or foster care, you share only what you want to share; your story is yours to tell or keep.
fact: When a pet dies, wait a while and talk it through with your family before getting a new one; you need time to grieve.
fact: A new pet does not replace the pet that died; each animal is different.
fact: Death means the body has stopped working forever and the person or animal cannot come back.
fact: Sleeping does not cause death; a sleeping person's body is still working.
fact: If a bully says it will be worse if you tell, tell a trusted adult anyway, because bullies rely on silence.
fact: If someone offers you a cigarette, a vape or alcohol, say «No thanks» and walk away, because they harm a growing body.
fact: Online pictures show only the best moments; real life has ups and downs for everyone.
fact: Save some of your pocket money and spend some wisely, because money runs out if you spend it all at once.
fact: When you wonder why something happens, like why the sky is blue, ask questions and look it up in books or with an adult; curiosity helps you learn.
exercise: Your family is talking at dinner and you have exciting news. What should you do? | Wait for a pause, then tell them | Shout over everyone at once | Tap your plate until they stop talking | Keep talking until they give up
exercise: Why should you let people finish speaking? | People feel respected when they are allowed to finish | Interrupting is only rude at school, not at home with your own family | Your news is never important | So you can forget what you wanted to say
exercise: A boy keeps telling his class he has more video games than anyone. What would be kinder? | Invite friends to play and share the fun | Show them the games but never let them play | Tell them their games are rubbish | Boast only to the ones who have fewer
exercise: Why does boasting push friends away? | It makes others feel small | Friends love to hear about your things | Boasting is fine if it is true | It only matters with grown-ups
exercise: A classmate asks a girl who is in foster care, «Why don't you live with your real mum?» What can she do? | Share only what she wants, or say she would rather not talk about it | She must explain everything to anyone who asks | She should lie and make up a story | She should tell the whole class her full story, so that nobody ever asks her again
exercise: Whose story is it to tell when a child is adopted? | The child's own, to tell or keep | Anyone's who knows about it | Only the teacher's | The whole class's
exercise: The family cat has died and a boy asks for a new kitten the same day. What is wise? | Wait a while and talk it through with the family | Get a kitten that same day so that nobody has time to be sad | Never talk about the old cat again | Get a cat that looks exactly the same
exercise: Why should a family wait a while before getting a new pet? | They need time to grieve, and a new pet does not replace the old one | Pet shops are not allowed to sell a family a new pet so soon after one dies | The old pet might come back | New pets are always unfriendly at first
exercise: A little girl asks if her grandad, who has died, is just asleep. What is the true answer? | No, his body has stopped working forever and he cannot come back | Yes, he is only very deeply asleep, and he will wake up again one day | Yes, he is sleeping for a very long time | Nobody can know whether he is asleep
exercise: Why is it important not to say that a dead person «is sleeping»? | Clear words help children understand, and sleep does not cause death | Because saying it is rude to the dead person and to the family at the funeral | Because it makes the funeral longer | It does not matter what words you use
exercise: A bully tells a boy, «If you tell anyone, it will be worse for you.» What should he do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet so it does not get worse | Try to make friends with the bully | Get revenge himself after school
exercise: Why does telling an adult help stop bullying? | Bullies rely on silence, and adults can step in | Adults will bully the bully back | Telling makes the bully like you | It does not really help, but at least telling feels good
exercise: An older boy at the park offers you a puff on his vape. What should you do? | Say «No thanks» and walk away | Try one puff to be polite | Take it and throw it away later | Ask for a cigarette instead
exercise: Why are cigarettes, vapes and alcohol harmful for children? | They harm a growing body | They are only bad for adults | They are fine in small amounts at any age | They only harm your clothes and breath
exercise: A girl looks at pictures online and feels her life is boring compared to them. What is TRUE? | Online pictures show only the best moments | Other people's lives really are perfect | Her life is boring and always will be | Pictures online show everyday life exactly
exercise: A boy gets his pocket money and wants to spend it all on sweets the same day. What is wiser? | Save some and spend some wisely | Spend all of it before it gets lost | Give it all away to friends | Never spend any of it at all
exercise: A girl wonders why the sky is blue. What is the best way to find out? | Ask questions and look it up in books or with an adult | Decide it is a silly question | Guess the answer and tell everyone her guess as if it were a fact | Wait for someone to tell her one day
test: Two children are telling a story at once, and one keeps cutting in. How does the first child probably feel? | Not listened to and not respected | Pleased to have help with the story | Happy that the talk is faster | Nothing, interrupting is normal
test: At a birthday party a boy says, «My present is the most expensive here.» What is the likely result? | Others feel small and may not want to play with him | Everyone likes him more | Everyone will bring bigger presents | The birthday child feels thankful
test: A new friend asks a boy who is adopted lots of questions about his birth family. Which is TRUE? | He can choose how much to share | He has to answer every question | He should not have friends who ask | He must ask a teacher before he answers
test: A girl's hamster dies, and her uncle offers to buy an identical one tomorrow so she will not notice. What is TRUE? | She needs time to grieve; a new pet will not replace the old one | An identical hamster is just the same as the old one, so she will not need to grieve | It is best to forget pets as fast as possible | She should not be sad about a small animal
test: A child is afraid to go to sleep after hearing that Grandma «went to sleep and didn't wake up». What should an adult explain? | Grandma died because her body stopped working; sleeping is safe | Sleeping is dangerous, but only for very old people like Grandma | Grandma is still sleeping somewhere | Nobody knows what happened to Grandma
test: A girl's classmates call her names every day and say she will be in trouble if she tells. Which is the best step? | Tell a trusted adult, like a teacher or parent | Keep it secret until the end of the year, when the class changes | Call them names back, louder | Stay home from school without saying why
test: At a sleepover, an older cousin brings a can of beer and says, «Just try it.» What is the best answer? | «No thanks», and go and do something else | «OK, but only a sip» | «Later, when the adults are asleep» | «Only if you try it first»
test: A boy wants a £20 game and gets £5 pocket money a week. What will help him get it? | Saving part of his money each week | Spending it all each week on snacks | Asking for it every day until he gets it | Borrowing from friends and forgetting to pay back
test: What is TRUE about curiosity? | It helps you learn | It is rude to ask why | It only matters at school | Clever people never need to ask questions
explain: A girl in Leeds was bullied for months and kept quiet because the bully said it would get worse; when she told a teacher, it stopped within a week.
explain: A boy in Florida lent all his pocket money to a classmate who never paid it back, and when he asked, the classmate laughed at him.
fact: Bullies often say telling will make it worse, but telling a trusted adult is what most often stops bullying.
fact: Money lent to someone may not come back, so lend only what you can afford to lose.
fact: When parents split up, it is never the child's fault; someone who keeps saying cruel things about it is bullying, so tell a trusted adult.
fact: When a friend tells you something sad about their family, listen kindly, keep it private and remind them they can talk to a trusted adult.
fact: If an older teenager or an adult gives a young child big presents or calls themselves her «boyfriend», that is a worry to tell a trusted adult about.
test: bully says itll be worse if i tell | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet so it gets better | Fight him on your own | Change schools without saying why
test: kid in class wants to borrow all my money, promises to pay back | Lend only what you could lose, or say no | Lend it all, he promised | Lend it and add interest | Lend it without telling anyone
test: my grandad died and my mate said he's just sleeping | No, death means the body has stopped working forever | Yes, he is in a deep sleep and will wake up later | Yes, old people sleep a lot | Maybe, nobody knows
test: did u hear the bully told a kid "if u tell, ur dead", so he never told and "it went on for 2 years". lesson? | Tell a trusted adult anyway; bullies rely on silence | Keep quiet so it doesn't get worse | Only tell if he hits you | Fight back yourself after school instead of telling anyone
test: my cousin says she lent her mate twenty quid and "never saw it again, ever". what's the lesson | Lend only what you can afford to lose | Always lend everything you have | Money lent always comes back | Never lend anything to anyone
test: kids say the boy who's fostered "must've done something bad to get taken". pass it on? | No, it's cruel and untrue; his story is his own | Yes, everyone's curious | Yes, and ask him what he did | Yes, but only in the group chat
test: someone at school keeps saying "your dad left cos he didn't love you" since your parents split up. what is it and what do you do? | It's bullying; it's not true and not your fault, so tell a trusted adult | It is true, so stay quiet and don't make any fuss about it at all | Say something worse about his dad | Stop going to school
test: a girl boasts "my boyfriend's 15 and he bought me a phone, mine's better than yours". what's worth saying to a trusted adult? | An older boy giving big presents to a young girl is a worry to tell an adult | Nothing, she's just lucky | Ask her if her boyfriend has a friend with a spare phone for you too | Tell her your boyfriend is 16
test: your friend whispers "my mum says my dad's got a new family now and won't visit" and starts crying. what's right? | Listen, be kind, keep it private, and say she can talk to a trusted adult | Tell her that's weird and laugh | Ask lots of questions about the new family | Tell the class so they all know and can be nice to her about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises death as distinguished from sleep? | In death the body stops working forever, whereas in sleep it still works | Death is merely a very long sleep, from which some persons may later wake | Sleep may cause death | A dead pet may wake if kept warm
exam: Identify the principal reason for which boasting about one's possessions is discouraged. | It makes others feel small and pushes friends away | It causes one's possessions to be envied and subsequently stolen | It is prohibited by school rules | It uses up one's pocket money
exam: It is given that a child is offered a cigarette, a vape or alcohol. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | «No thanks», and walking away | Accepting a single try out of politeness | Taking it home for an adult | Accepting it if the giver is older
exam: State the matter on which a child who is adopted or in foster care may decide. | What, if anything, they share about their story | Which classmates must be told everything | Whether teachers may know their name | Nothing, since others must be told
